<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arthur Wellesley, 1st Duke of Wellington</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1769">1769</a>–<a href="/years/1852">1852</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Duke of Wellington, Arthur Wellesley</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arthur Wellesley, 1st Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a>, was a British general and statesman. After campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, he commanded the British and Portuguese armies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a> from 1808, driving the French out of Portugal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> by 1814. In 1815 he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a> with the Prussians, and he later served twice as prime minister.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arthur-wellesley-1st-duke-of-wellington.jpg" alt="Arthur Wellesley, 1st Duke of Wellington" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Lawrence · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Arthur_Wellesley,_1st_Duke_of_Wellington.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Arthur Wellesley was born into an Anglo-Irish family of the Protestant Ascendancy, as the Hon. Arthur Wesley, the sixth of nine children of Garret Wesley, 1st Earl of Mornington, and Anne, Countess of Mornington. The exact date and place of his birth are not known; biographers mostly follow newspaper evidence that he was born on 1 May 1769, the day before his baptism at St. Peter's Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>.</p><p>The family was short of money, and on his mother's advice his brother Richard asked the Duke of Rutland, then Lord Lieutenant of Ireland, to consider Arthur for an army commission. He was gazetted ensign in the 73rd Regiment of Foot on 7 March 1787 and became aide-de-camp to successive lords lieutenant, in a post whose duties were mainly social. In 1798 he changed the spelling of his surname to Wellesley.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> in February 1797, and his brother Richard, Lord Mornington, soon became Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-anglo-mysore-war">Fourth Anglo-Mysore War</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> he ended the conflict with a victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangapatna">Seringapatam</a> in 1799 and was appointed governor of Seringapatam and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>. As a major general he won a decisive victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-confederacy">Maratha Confederacy</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-assaye">Battle of Assaye</a> in 1803. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a> he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a> on 12 July 1808 and defeated the French at Roliça and Vimeiro. Recalled to face a Court of Enquiry after the Convention of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sintra">Sintra</a>, which he had not signed, he was cleared. He was promoted field marshal after the victory at Vitoria in 1813.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s first exile in 1814 he served as ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and was made Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a>. When Napoleon escaped from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elba">Elba</a> on 26 February 1815, Wellington took command of a British-German and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Blücher</a>'s Prussians. After Ligny and Quatre Bras he withdrew to a ridge south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a>, where on 18 June he met Napoleon for the first time. He was prime minister from 1828 to 1830 and for under a month in 1834, oversaw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-catholic-relief-act-1829">Roman Catholic Relief Act 1829</a> and strongly opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reform-act-1832">Reform Act 1832</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a> died at Walmer Castle in Kent on 14 September 1852, aged 83, of the after-effects of a stroke. He was given a state funeral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 18 November 1852, one of a small number of British subjects so honoured. The nickname Iron Duke relates to his political career, and its use is often disparaging; it may date from the iron shutters fitted at Apsley House in 1832 against rioters. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> called him a Sepoy General, while Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> called him the conqueror of the world's conqueror.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arthur Wellesley, 1st Duke of Wellington” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Horatio Nelson’s victory at the Battle of Trafalgar in 1805 ended the threat of French invasion, and the <b>Duke of Wellington</b>’s campaigns in the Peninsular War and his victory, with Prussian forces, at the Battle of Waterloo in 1815 helped bring down Napoleon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Some 12,000 government troops, most of them militia or yeomanry, were involved, a larger number, Eric Hobsbawm wrote, than the army the <b>Duke of Wellington</b> led into Portugal in 1808 during the Peninsular War.</blockquote><a href="/movement/luddites">Luddites · Outcomes</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. Died in Walmer Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.35,-6.26&amp;year=1822" aria-label="Dublin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4411 329 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Arthur Wellesley, 1st Duke of Wellington"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4861 599h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4861 599h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.35,-6.26&amp;year=1822">What was here in 1822? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Never did I see such a pounding match. Both were what the boxers call &quot;gluttons.&quot; Napoleon did not manoeuvre at all. He just moved forward in the old style, in columns, and was driven off in the old style.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Marshal Beresford after Waterloo, July 1815 &#183; original &#183; from Maxims and Opinions of the Duke of Wellington (1845) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15254/pg15254-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…if I could avoid, by any sacrifice whatever, even one month of civil war in the country to which I was attached, I would sacrifice my life in order to do it.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech in the House of Lords on Catholic emancipation, 2 April 1829 &#183; original &#183; from Maxims and Opinions of the Duke of Wellington (1845) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15254/pg15254-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;They are shifting their objects; and, having got their cake, they want both to eat it and keep it.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter after Waterloo, 1815, on the allies and the British government &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15254/pg15254-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I have likewise said that, as a private friend, I advised him to have nothing to do with so foul a transaction…&#8221;</p><footer>Letter, 26 June 1815, on the Prussian wish to put Napoleon to death &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15254/pg15254-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I say, there is nothing which destroys property, eats up prosperity by the roots, and demoralizes the character, to the degree that civil war does…&#8221;</p><footer>Speech in the House of Lords on Catholic emancipation, 2 April 1829 &#183; original &#183; from Maxims and Opinions of the Duke of Wellington (1845) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15254/pg15254-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
